WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.875 --> 00:00:03.500
We present GS-ProCams,

2
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.875
the first Gaussian splatting-based framework

3
00:00:05.875 --> 00:00:08.041
for projector-camera systems (ProCams).

4
00:00:08.916 --> 00:00:11.750
GS-ProCams significantly enhances the efficiency

5
00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:14.458
of projection mapping which requires establishing

6
00:00:14.458 --> 00:00:16.666
geometric and photometric mappings

7
00:00:16.666 --> 00:00:19.041
between the projector and the camera.

8
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:27.666
Projector-camera systems (ProCams) are integrated systems

9
00:00:27.666 --> 00:00:30.375
 that combine a projector and a camera.

10
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.166
Geometric and photometric mappings between  

11
00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:35.291
the projector and the camera are foundational

12
00:00:35.291 --> 00:00:37.666
to ProCams and serve as cornerstones

13
00:00:37.666 --> 00:00:39.541
 for numerous applications.

14
00:00:39.916 --> 00:00:42.625
However, most existing methods are designed

15
00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:44.708
for a specific camera viewpoint.

16
00:00:45.333 --> 00:00:47.750
If these viewpoint specific methods are applied

17
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.375
at a novel camera viewpoint, it will be necessary

18
00:00:50.375 --> 00:00:52.750
to acquire data and train the model again

19
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:54.958
 to establish the new mapping relationships.

20
00:00:56.083 --> 00:00:57.166
The state-of-the-art approach

21
00:00:57.166 --> 00:00:59.666
 for view-agnostic projection mapping employs

22
00:00:59.666 --> 00:01:02.583
 multi-layer perceptrons (MLPs) to represent scenes.

23
00:01:03.291 --> 00:01:06.333
However, this method presents significant challenges

24
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:08.708
 in terms of computational resources,

25
00:01:08.708 --> 00:01:11.041
as evidenced by its slow training speed,

26
00:01:11.416 --> 00:01:12.833
 limited inference efficiency,

27
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:14.583
and high memory demands.

28
00:01:15.416 --> 00:01:18.125
To address this issue, we propose GS-ProCams,

29
00:01:18.125 --> 00:01:21.375
 that employs 2D Gaussian for scene representations.

30
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.041
Since standard 2D Gaussian Splatting cannot capture

31
00:01:25.041 --> 00:01:27.625
 the interaction between the projector's illumination

32
00:01:27.625 --> 00:01:28.583
 and the surface,

33
00:01:28.583 --> 00:01:30.750
we enhance 2D Gaussian Splatting

34
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.083
using BRDF parameterization.

35
00:01:33.083 --> 00:01:36.375
We obtain the geometry and material of the target surface,

36
00:01:36.375 --> 00:01:38.208
through differentiable splatting.

37
00:01:38.666 --> 00:01:41.666
Then, we simulate real-world projector-camera systems,

38
00:01:41.666 --> 00:01:43.625
using physically-based rendering.

39
00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:46.958
GS-ProCams thus efficiently establishes

40
00:01:46.958 --> 00:01:49.083
 the geometric and photometric mappings 

41
00:01:49.083 --> 00:01:51.041
between the projector and the camera.

42
00:01:51.875 --> 00:01:53.166
After training on a scene,

43
00:01:53.166 --> 00:01:55.541
GS-ProCams can be directly applied

44
00:01:55.541 --> 00:01:57.458
at novel camera viewpoints.

45
00:02:00.583 --> 00:02:02.583
To train GS-ProCams for a scene,

46
00:02:02.583 --> 00:02:05.333
we project various patterns onto the target surface

47
00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:09.083
and capture images from multiple viewpoints using a camera.

48
00:02:09.541 --> 00:02:12.000
This includes a fixed pattern for each viewpoint,

49
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.416
such as a digital speckle pattern to determine

50
00:02:14.416 --> 00:02:17.125
the positions of the projector and camera viewpoints,

51
00:02:17.125 --> 00:02:23.166
and a few nature images for model training.

52
00:02:31.125 --> 00:02:32.458
After training on a scene,

53
00:02:32.458 --> 00:02:35.083
GS-ProCams can simulate the target surface

54
00:02:35.083 --> 00:02:36.333
 with a novel projection

55
00:02:36.333 --> 00:02:38.208
at a viewpoint that GS-ProCams

56
00:02:38.208 --> 00:02:40.083
has not seen previously.

57
00:02:42.791 --> 00:02:44.541
This enables various applications

58
00:02:44.541 --> 00:02:46.291
of projector-camera systems.

59
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:51.083
For example, because GS-ProCams is fully differentiable,

60
00:02:51.083 --> 00:02:52.666
 it allows GS-ProCams

61
00:02:52.666 --> 00:02:54.416
to treat projector compensation

62
00:02:54.416 --> 00:02:56.916
as an inverse rendering problem.

63
00:02:57.458 --> 00:03:00.166
By aligning the simulated result of GS-ProCams

64
00:03:00.166 --> 00:03:01.791
with a desired image,

65
00:03:01.791 --> 00:03:03.625
we can achieve projector compensation

66
00:03:03.625 --> 00:03:06.250
for this viewpoint in the real world.

67
00:03:09.333 --> 00:03:12.291
Due to its efficiency, our framework can be combined

68
00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:15.000
with popular large generative models to achieve innovative

69
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.166
spatial augmented reality applications.

70
00:03:19.291 --> 00:03:21.166
For instance, it can be integrated with

71
00:03:21.333 --> 00:03:23.750
an inpainting model to eliminate objects

72
00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:25.291
in the real world.

73
00:03:32.625 --> 00:03:35.333
Similarly, we can combine with diffusion models

74
00:03:35.333 --> 00:03:37.333
to change the appearance of reality

75
00:03:37.333 --> 00:03:39.375
through text prompts.

76
00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:50.958
Thanks for watching.

77
00:03:51.958 --> 00:03:55.458
(See the principal manuscript for details)

